# SET-PIECE: the watchpost. A PATCH, and the one that carries a gun.
#
# Three units of high ground on a footprint two cells wide, with a TURRET on top
# and a LADDER as the only way to it. Everything about it is the trade between
# those two facts.
#
# THE LADDER IS THE POINT, not a decoration. A ramp spends a row per unit of
# climb, so three units is three rows and a broad approach; a ladder does any
# height in ONE cell. That is what makes a tall tower fit in a patch at all --
# and `SimConfig.CLIMB_SPEED` says what it costs: the climb is slower than
# walking and it happens somewhere you cannot dodge, shove or shoot. Taking this
# post means three seconds of being a target with no verbs.
#
# WHICH IS WHY THE TURRET HAS TO BE ANSWERABLE FROM THE GROUND, and it is: M23
# phase 0 made `point` the shipped aim mode, so a player can tilt a shot up at it
# rather than walking until it is level with them. Under the old level aim this
# piece would have been a gun you cannot shoot back at, which is the
# hazard-with-no-counter shape CLAUDE.md warns about -- climbing the ladder would
# have been the ONLY answer, and the ladder is the one place you cannot fight.
#
# THE TURRET IS AUTHORED, NOT DRESSED. Layer 3 keeps out of piece rows entirely,
# and rightly: the empty cells of a composition are part of it. What sits on the
# post is the composition's own business, which is what makes this a set-piece
# rather than a shape with a hazard budget spent near it.
#
# Edge rows level with the terrain, and `piece_exit = 0`: terrain runs past a
# patch on both sides at the plateau it was stamped at.

name = piece_watchpost
base_height = 0
width = 3
length = 5
piece_exit = 0
tags = foot, piece, enemy

